pen({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/307\",\"lineno\":25}); Total lines: 75\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2026/307\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Composition Theorems for Zero-Knowledge IOPs】\nL32: \nL33: Himanshu Vashishth, Bar-Ilan University\nL34: \nL35: Mor Weiss, Bar-Ilan University\nL36: ##### Abstract\nL37: Interactive Oracle Proofs (IOPs) enable a probabilistic verifier interacting with a prover to verify NP statements while reading only few bits from the prover messages. Zero-Knowledge IOPs (ZK-IOPs) have the additional guarantee that a query-bounded (possibly malicious) verifier learns nothing about the NP witness. We initiate a systematic study of ZK preservation under IOP composition, and prove general composition theorems for ZK-IOPs in the 2- and multi-IOP setting.\nL38: Our main result shows that ZK is preserved in the setting of perfect, black-box, straight-line ZK (the standard setting for ZK-IOPs), if the outer IOP has an additional mild property that is satisfied by existing ZK-IOPs. Contrary to common belief, this does not follow from composition theorems for multiparty protocols (Kushilevitz, Lindell and Rabin, STOC`06).\nL39: Our composition theorems show that ZK-IOPs can be modularly designed by composing sub-protocols, and ZK of the composed system follows seamlessly from the ZK guarantees of its building blocks.\nL40: Using our composition theorems, we easily derive both new and known results on ZK-IOPs in various settings, including ZK preservation under parallel/sequential composition, ZK of IOPs for sumcheck and codeswitching, ZK of IOPs for NP using arithmetization and sumcheck, and ZK preservation under IOP proof composition (reproving a result of Bootle, Chiesa and Liu, EC`22).\nL41: ##### Metadata\nL42: \nL43: Available format(s)\nL44:     【17†PDF】\nL45: Category\nL46:     【18†Foundations】\nL47: Publication info\nL48:     Preprint.\nL49: Keywords\nL50:     【19†Interactive Oracle Proofs】【20†Zero Knowledge】【21†Composition】【22†Sumcheck】【23†Code Switching】\nL51: Contact author(s)\nL52:     himanshu18235 @ iiitd ac in\nL53: mormorweiss @ gmail com\nL54: History\nL55:     2026-05-25: revised\nL56:     2026-02-18: received\nL57:     【24†See all versions】\nL58: Short URL\nL59:     【25†https://ia.cr/2026/307†ia.cr】\nL60: License\nL61: \nL62: 【26†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL63: BibTeX 【27†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL64: \nL65:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2026/307,\nL66:           author = {Himanshu Vashishth and Mor Weiss},\nL67:           title = {Composition Theorems for Zero-Knowledge {IOPs}},\nL68:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2026/307},\nL69:           year = {2026},\nL70:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/307}\nL71:     }\nL72: \nL73: \nL74: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPepper: toward practical verifiable computation (https://www.pepper-project.org/)\nciteturn4view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.pepper-project.org/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 294\nL0:   * 【0†Home】\nL1:   * 【1†About】\nL2:     * 【1†What is Pepper?】\nL3:     * 【2†Approach and research】\nL4:     * 【3†Summary of results】\nL5:     * 【4†Built systems】\nL6:     * 【5†Performance】\nL7:   * 【6†Publications】\nL8:   * 【7†Presentations】\nL9:   * 【8†Tutorials and exercises】\nL10:   * 【9†People】\nL11:   * 【10†Related projects】\nL12:   * 【11†Source code】\nL13:   * 【12†Funding and support】\nL14:   * 【13†Contact】\nL15: ## Elevator pitch\nL16: \nL17: The Pepper project at NYU and UT Austin is tackling the problem of verifying outsourced computations. Our approach is to reduce to practice powerful tools from complexity theory and cryptography: probabilistically checkable proofs (PCPs), efficient arguments, etc.\nL18: \nL19: Highlights of the project’s results include:\nL20:   * improving the performance of a PCP-based 【14†efficient argument†www.cs.ucla.edu】 by a factor of 10^{20}\nL21:   * broadening the computational model used in verifiable computation: from Boolean circuits to a general-purpose model\nL22:   * extending verifiability to representative uses of cloud computing: MapReduce jobs, simple database queries, interactions with private state, etc.\nL23:   * a series of 【11†built】 【4†systems】 that implement the aforementioned refinements, with the following features:\nL24:     * GPU acceleration\nL25:     * a compiler that allows the developer to express a computation in a restricted subset of C; the compiler produces executables that implement the protocol entities\nL26:   * Verifiable ASICs, a new approach to building trustworthy hardware using untrusted components\nL27: Though these systems 【5†are not yet at true practicality】, they are arguably practical in some regimes. More importantly, these systems highlight the potential applicability of the theory, and indeed, there is now 【10†a thriving research area】 based on this promise. Thus, we are hopeful that over the next few years, the underlying theoretical tools will find their way into real systems.\nL28: ### Overview: what is Pepper?\nL29: \nL30: Pepper is an academic research project whose goal is to make verified computation practical. By verified computation (which is sometimes also called “verifiable computation”), we mean a system that implements the following picture:\nL31: \nL32: 【15†Image: Overview of Pepper】\nL33: \nL34: One motivation is cloud computing (more generally, third-party computing). These environments bring:\nL35:   * Issues of scale: with hundreds of thousands or millions of machines, correct and unfailing execution in all cases seems unlikely.\nL36:   * Issues of trust: the computation “provider” and the “consumer” are different entities.\nL37: \nL38: Goal: A system that is\nL39:   1. Comprehensive -- the system makes no assumptions about the behavior and capabilities of the performing computer other than cryptographic hardness.\nL40:   2. General-purpose -- not specialized to a particular family of functions.\nL41:   3. Practical -- can be used for real problems.\nL42: \nL43: Prior work does not meet all three of these properties.\nL44: \nL45: We approach this problem using deep results in cryptography and complexity theory. 【2†Here】 is a high-level description of the foundations of our approach.\nL46: ### Our approach: PCPs, argument systems, and interactive proofs\nL47: \nL48: Our approach is to apply the fascinating theory of Probabilistically Checkable Proofs (PCPs) and two closely related constructs: argument systems and interactive proofs.\nL49: The theory surrounding PCPs demonstrates, astonishingly, that it is possible (in principle) to check a mathematical proof by investigating only a few bits of that proof. In our context, this implies that the 【1†picture we wanted】 has a solution, one that is unconditional and general-purpose.\nL50: ### The research: make the theory practical\nL51: \nL52: Unfortunately, PCPs, as described in the theoretical literature, are not remotely practical: in the most asymptotically “efficient” schemes, the server would take trillions of years to produce a proof.\nL53: \nL54: Can we incorporate PCPs into a built system? So far, we have been able to come close. 【3†Here】 is a top-level summary of Pepper’s results to date.\nL55: ### Summary of Pepper’s results so far\nL56: \nL57: Our line of work on 【16†Pepper】, 【17†Ginger】, and 【18†Zaatar】 has instantiated an argument system (an interactive protocol that assumes a computationally bounded prover) due to Ishai, Kushilevitz, and Ostrovsky (CCC 2007), in 【19†this paper†ieeexplore.ieee.org】 (also available 【14†here†www.cs.ucla.edu】). Through theoretical and practical refinements, we have reduced the cost of this argument system by a factor of roughly 10^{20} (seriously).\nL58: In another line of work, 【20†Allspice】, we have built on an 【21†interactive proof system†dl.acm.org】 due to Goldwasser, Kalai, and Rothblum (STOC 2008), and 【22†refined and implemented†arxiv.org】 by Cormode, Mitzenmacher, and Thaler (ITCS 2012). One of the chief advantages of this line of work is that it avoids expensive cryptographic operations.\nL59: One might wonder: which protocol is “best”? Our experience has been that it depends on the computation. Accordingly, Allspice includes a compiler that takes as input a high-level programming language and performs static analysis to compile to the best available protocol for that computation.\nL60: In the above works, the computational model does not support stateful computations, namely those that work over RAM, or computations for which the verifier does not have the full input. In 【23†Pantry】, we overcome this limitation, and apply the verification machinery to MapReduce jobs, queries against remote databases, and applications for which the prover’s state is private.\nL61: \nL62: 【4†Here】 is a system-by-system description.\nL63: ### Built systems under the Pepper project\nL64: \nL65: In the descriptions below, the prover is the entity that performs a computation. The verifier checks a proof (produced by the prover) that the computation was performed correctly.\nL66: \nL67: Pepper\nL68: 【16†Pepper】 is the first system to challenge the folklore that PCP-derived machinery is impractical. Pepper implements an efficient argument system based on a protocol of 【14†Ishai et al., CCC 2007†www.cs.ucla.edu】, and incorporates new theoretical work to improve performance by 20 orders of magnitude. Pepper is implemented and experimentally evaluated, and is arguably practical for some computations and in some regimes.\n"
